The lower tail of Student-t with one degree of freedom is a Cauchy probability. At −10⁹ the CDF returns zero and its logarithm returns −Inf, although both values are representable.

```julia
using StatsFuns
x = 1e9
p = atan(inv(x)) / π
@show tdistcdf(1.0, -x) p           # expected ≈ 3.183098861837907e-10
@show tdistlogcdf(1.0, -x) log(p)
@show fdistccdf(1.0, 1.0, x^2)     # expected ≈ 2p
```

F and Student-t share the F-to-beta transformation. When the transformed variate rounds to one, the small complementary argument is lost. The fix evaluates that complement directly and exchanges the beta parameters and tail. This applies to the forward probabilities and their logarithms.
